  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1223 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nSunday will be another cool and dreary day, with a cold air mass \nbeing further reinforced by a cold front while the upper level \nsystem exits to our southeast. Wrap around moisture will likely \nproduce some lingering showers Sunday morning - afternoon (30-60% \nPOPs), favoring central and eastern counties of the CWA. Even though \nrain should gradually clear by Sunday night, cloud cover and north \nwinds will keep highs on the chilly side in the 40s. Monday morning, \nwe could be waking up to near freezing temperatures across the \nregion.\n\nModels then depict ridging replacing the upper level low next week, \nkicking off a warming trend back to near average temperatures Mon - \nThur (highs in the 50s & 60s). Surface winds should be breezy again \nduring this time frame thanks to a persistently tight pressure \ngradient, especially across the northwest combined Panhandles Tue & \nWed (40-70% chance for gusts >40 mph). Track and timing of systems \ncontinue to shift around amongst long range datasets, but there is \nstill a signal for an active pattern to potentially return late next \nweek. We will have to wait and see if any of these hypothetical \nsystems provide a significant cool down. In the meantime, it appears \nas though we should continue to reap the benefits of El Nino, \nmeaning it's more likely than not that any system we receive should \nprovide some moisture. light showers move in\n\nHarrel",
